VM snapshots (savevm/loadvm) of the Compact 7 image fail on stock QEMU 8.2
with

  VQ 0 size 0x100 < last_avail_idx 0x41ad - used_idx 0x0
  Failed to load virtio-blk:virtio

The image's virtio-mmio drivers use the modern register layout (hence
-global virtio-mmio.force-legacy=false) but only negotiate the low 32
feature bits, so VIRTIO_F_VERSION_1 is never acknowledged. On loadvm QEMU
then treats the queues as legacy and recomputes the avail/used ring
addresses from the descriptor address, although the real addresses were
migrated (the virtqueues subsection is written whenever the *host* offers
VERSION_1). Trusting the migrated addresses in that case fixes it.

Apply to QEMU 8.2 (hw/virtio/virtio.c, virtio_load()), build arm-softmmu
and put the binary at Emulator/work/qemu/qemu-system-arm (or set QEMU=...);
run-emulator.sh picks it up. Then start-vw.sh --save vw / --resume vw work.

Related, handled without a patch: the machine must run with
secure=off,virtualization=off (run-emulator.sh does), because QEMU 8.2 does
not migrate the secure-bank TTBR1/DACR/CONTEXTIDR an EL3-capable CPU uses
when the guest never leaves secure state, as this kernel never does.

--- a/hw/virtio/virtio.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/virtio.c
@@ -3119,7 +3119,13 @@ int virtio_load(VirtIODevice *vdev, QEMUFile *f, int version_id)
              * to calculate used and avail ring addresses based on the desc
              * address.
              */
-            if (virtio_vdev_has_feature(vdev, VIRTIO_F_VERSION_1)) {
+            /*
+             * The ring addresses are migrated whenever the host offers
+             * VERSION_1 (see virtio_virtqueue_needed), so use them even if
+             * the guest never acknowledged the feature bit.
+             */
+            if (virtio_host_has_feature(vdev, VIRTIO_F_VERSION_1)) {
                 virtio_init_region_cache(vdev, i);
             } else {
                 virtio_queue_update_rings(vdev, i);
